---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';
import RepoExplorer from '../components/RepoExplorer.astro';
import Dial from '../components/Dial.astro';
import FailFirst from '../components/FailFirst.astro';
import Conveyor from '../components/Conveyor.astro';
import { written, computed } from '../data/site';
import { url, guideUrl } from '../lib/links';
import { chapters } from '../lib/guide';
const all = await chapters();
const first = all[0]!;
const count = all.length;
---
<Base path="/">
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <div>
        <h1><Logo /><span class="sr">CanonBall</span><span class="wm" aria-hidden="true"><b>Canon</b><i>Ball</i></span></h1>
        <p class="tagline">The lifecycle <span class="a">agents</span> can run and <span class="p">people</span> can trust.</p>
        <p class="lede">CanonBall is a method for building and operating software with AI agents. People write down what the system must do. Agents build it and produce the evidence. Every release ships all three together.</p>
        <div class="cta">
          <a class="btn" href="#workflow">See how work moves</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href={guideUrl(first.id)}>Read the guide</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="ballfig">
        <div class="ballrow">
          <Logo title="The CanonBall mark" />
          <ul>
            <li class="p"><b>Canon</b><span>what the system promises</span></li>
            <li class="a"><b>Build</b><span>the code that keeps the promises</span></li>
            <li class="v"><b>Verification Record</b><span>the evidence that it does</span></li>
            <li class="r"><b>Release</b><span>all three, shipped together</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figcaption>One turn of the work. People agree the canon, agents build, the Verification Record is computed, and a CanonBall leaves the loop as a release.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="band" id="why">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why now</p>
        <h2 class="t">Writing software got cheap. Knowing what it does did not.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="why">
        <div><b>Agents write faster than people can read.</b><span>Reviewing every change wastes the agent. Skipping review turns each change into a risk nobody measured.</span></div>
        <div><b>So the question changed.</b><span>It used to be "can we build this?". Now it is "what does this change do, and what does the system still promise afterwards?"</span></div>
        <div><b>The effort moves to verification.</b><span>Most of the engineering now goes into showing that a promise holds. CanonBall organizes the work around that.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="cap"><a class="more" href={guideUrl('why')}>Why the work changed</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="workflow">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">How work moves</p>
        <h2 class="t">A milestone goes in. A CanonBall comes out.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Requests gather into a milestone. People and agents shape it together, agents build it and prove it, an agent reviews it, and it ships as a CanonBall. Three things on this belt are not in a conventional software lifecycle. They are numbered.</p>
      </div>
      <Conveyor />
      <p class="cta" style="margin-top:26px"><a class="btn" href={guideUrl('workflow')}>Walk it, station by station</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="primitives">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">What gets written down</p>
        <h2 class="t">People steer agents with a few written things.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Each one is a short file in plain language, and most are written at the Shape station. Together they are the controls: an agent may do anything that respects them, and nothing that does not trace back to them.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="group"><span class="pill p">People write</span></p>
      <div class="prims">
        {written.map((w) => (
          <article class="prim">
            <h3>{w.name}</h3>
            <p class="what">{w.what}</p>
            <p class="ex">{w.example}</p>
            <p class="whyx"><b>Why it exists.</b> {w.why}</p>
            <a class="more" href={guideUrl(w.slug)}>Learn more<span class="sr"> about {w.name}</span></a>
          </article>
        ))}
        <aside class="prim note">
          <h3>Five of these are clauses</h3>
          <p class="what">A promise, an invariant, a decision, a constraint and an assumption are each a kind of <b>clause</b>: one file with an ID and its examples.</p>
          <p class="what">The approved clauses together are the <b>canon</b>.</p>
        </aside>
      </div>
      <p class="group"><span class="pill v">Machines answer</span></p>
      <div class="prims">
        {computed.map((c) => (
          <article class="prim m">
            <h3>{c.name}</h3>
            <p class="what">{c.what}</p>
            <p class="whyx"><b>Why it exists.</b> {c.why}</p>
            <a class="more" href={guideUrl(c.slug)}>Learn more<span class="sr"> about {c.name}</span></a>
          </article>
        ))}
        <aside class="prim note">
          <h3>Nobody writes these</h3>
          <p class="what">They are produced by running checks and by a program that reads the results. That is what makes them worth trusting.</p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="structure">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The repository</p>
        <h2 class="t">Every station writes to its own place.</h2>
        <p class="sub">The repository is laid out by the work. Each folder answers one question and has one kind of writer. Follow milestone M2 through it one station at a time, or open any folder.</p>
      </div>
      <RepoExplorer />
      <p class="cap">The example is a refunds service. Dots show who writes each file: <span class="p">people</span>, <span class="a">agents that build</span>, <span class="v">agents and programs that verify</span>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="verification">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Verification engineering</p>
        <h2 class="t"><span class="v">Verification engineering</span> is where the work is now.</h2>
        <p class="sub">When people wrote the code, most of the effort went into the product and testing came second. With agents writing it, that ratio turns over. The discipline that matters is engineering the environments in which a promise can be checked, and designing them with a person. People can trust evidence only if they understand how it is produced.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="two">
        <div class="box">
          <h3>A verification environment has seven parts</h3>
          <ul class="anat">
            <li><b>Boundary</b><span>What is under test: the refunds service, at this commit.</span></li>
            <li><b>Stimulus</b><span>How it is driven: requests through the public interface.</span></li>
            <li><b>World</b><span>What surrounds it: a real local store, a fake payment gateway.</span></li>
            <li><b>Probes</b><span>What can be observed: what the store holds, what the gateway paid.</span></li>
            <li><b>Oracle</b><span>What decides pass or fail: a mechanical check, not an opinion.</span></li>
            <li><b>Coverage</b><span>What was exercised, and which injected faults were caught.</span></li>
            <li><b>Control</b><span>Seed, clock, a clean start and a clean teardown.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <div class="cannot"><b>It also states what it cannot show.</b> Real gateway timing, for one. Evidence counts only inside what the environment can show.</div>
        </div>
        <FailFirst />
      </div>
      <p class="cap"><a class="more" href={guideUrl('verification-engineering')}>Verification engineering</a> &nbsp; <a class="more" href={guideUrl('environments')}>Verification environments</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="observability">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Observability</p>
        <h2 class="t">Agents work by looking. Give them something to see.</h2>
        <p class="sub">A person can debug on intuition and a dashboard. An agent cannot. Shown what the system did, it finds the cause in minutes. Left without it, it guesses and patches the symptom. So observability is built first, and built for the agents.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="two">
        <div class="box">
          <h3>Three rules</h3>
          <ul class="anat">
            <li><b>Decisions</b><span>Every decision the system makes is logged with the clause it applied and the inputs it used.</span></li>
            <li><b>Traces</b><span>One trace follows each request through every adapter.</span></li>
            <li><b>Tools</b><span>Agents have commands to read logs, traces, metrics and state, and to replay a request.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <div class="cannot"><b>One investment, three uses.</b> The builder sees what its code does. The verifier explains every failure. The on-call agent diagnoses an incident. People see the same view.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="box">
          <h3>What the on-call agent saw</h3>
          <pre class="obs">$ observe decisions --order 5512

REQ-012  refund window
  result        refused
  reason        day 31 of 30
  counted from  dispatch, 2 March
  delivered     4 March</pre>
          <p class="obsnote">A customer was refused on day 29. The log shows why without anyone guessing: the window was counted from dispatch, and the vocabulary says delivery.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="cap"><a class="more" href={guideUrl('observability')}>Observability: letting agents see</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="architecture">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">A recommended architecture</p>
        <h2 class="t">Keep decisions apart from side effects.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Agents do their best work inside clear structure. CanonBall does not dictate how your code is laid out. It recommends one habit, because it makes evidence cheap: put the logic that decides in one place, and the code that touches the outside world in another.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="arch">
        <div class="z"><b>Inputs come in as data</b>The request, the current time, a reading from a sensor. The logic never fetches them itself.</div>
        <em aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</em>
        <div class="z core"><b>Decision logic: the functional core</b>Given this state and this input, what should happen? No database, no network, no clock. The same inputs always give the same answer.<br /><span class="pill a">checked for every input</span></div>
        <em aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</em>
        <div class="z"><b>Adapters: the imperative shell</b>Paying out, sending an email, writing a row. Each goes through one named place.<br /><span class="pill p">people still review this code</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="aka">
        <div><b>You may already know it.</b>Gary Bernhardt named it functional core, imperative shell. Alistair Cockburn's ports and adapters is the same idea.</div>
        <div><b>Why it suits agents.</b>Logic with no side effects can be tested exhaustively, replayed exactly, and rewritten safely.</div>
        <div><b>What people keep.</b>The interface, the constraints, the adapters, and any design choice they promote to a decision.</div>
      </div>
      <p class="cap"><a class="more" href={guideUrl('architecture')}>An architecture agents can work in</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="dial">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Adoption</p>
        <h2 class="t">Hand over decisions as agents earn them.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Each kind of decision has its own level of automation. A team starts with agents recommending and people deciding. Settings move one at a time, on track record, and where they end up depends on the domain. Agent review stays on at every setting. What moves is how often a person looks as well.</p>
      </div>
      <Dial />
      <p class="cap">An illustration. Each team writes its own thresholds in advance. A new model release does not move a setting. Agreement with people over many cases does, and one failure that gets through moves it back. <a class="more" href={guideUrl('delegation')}>Handing over decisions</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="guide">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The guide</p>
        <h2 class="t">Read the rest in {count} short chapters.</h2>
        <p class="sub">This page is the overview. The guide follows one milestone of a refunds service through every station, then takes each concept in turn.</p>
        <p class="cta" style="margin-top:22px"><a class="btn" href={guideUrl(first.id)}>Start with chapter one</a><a class="btn ghost" href={guideUrl('workflow')}>Jump to the walkthrough</a><a class="btn ghost" href={url('/glossary/')}>Glossary</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
